{{also|häck|Hack}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/hæk/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-hack.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|æk|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|hacken}}, {{m|enm|hakken}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*haccian|gloss=to hack}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*hakkōn}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*hakkōną|t=to chop; hoe; hew}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*keg-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*keng-|t=to be sharp; peg; hook; handle}}.

Cognate with {{cog|stq|häkje|t=to hack}}, {{cog|fy|hakje|t=to hack}}, {{cog|nl|hakken|t=to chop up; hack}}, {{cog|de|hacken|t=to chop; hack; hoe}}, {{cog|da|hakke|t=to chop}}, {{cog|sv|hacka|t=to hack; chop}}, {{cog|fr|hacher|t=to chop}}.

The computer senses date back to at least 1955 when it initially referred to creative problem solving. By 1963, the negative connotations of “{{m|en|black hat}}” or malicious hacking had become associated with telephone hacking (cf. {{m|en|phreaking}}).<ref name="ny">{{cite-journal|journal={{w|New Yorker}} |title=A Short History of “Hack” |last=Yagoda |first=Ben |date=March 6, 2014|url=https://www.newyorker.com/tech/annals-of-technology/a-short-history-of-hack}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[chop]] or [[cut down]] in a rough manner. {{defdate|circa 12th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|They '''hacked''' the brush down and made their way through the jungle.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Tarzan of the Apes|chapter=6|passage=Among other things he found a sharp hunting knife, on the keen blade of which he immediately proceeded to cut his finger. Undaunted he continued his experiments, finding that he could '''hack''' and hew splinters of wood from the table and chairs with this new toy.}}
# To [[withstand]] or [[put up]] with a difficult situation. {{defdate|20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Can you '''hack''' it out here with no electricity or running water?}}
#* {{quote-web|en|title=The Agonizing Question: Is New York City Worth It Anymore?|work=New York Times|date=2020-06-05|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/05/nyregion/coronavirus-leaving-nyc.html|author=Alyson Krueger|passage=New Yorkers have been fleeing for months. But the fear some residents have of the violent reactions to the protests here is adding a new challenge to those asking themselves whether they can '''hack''' the city. Many are deciding not to return.}}
