===Etymology 3===
Abbreviation of {{m|en|hackney||an ordinary horse}}, probably from place name [[Hackney]].

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[horse]] for hire, especially one which is old and tired. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|page=84|volume=I|passage=A cold wind, a piercing rain, and a bad road, with a worse '''hack''' (for his own horses had been knocked up), rendered more acute the misery which he, as a parted lover, was bound to feel.}}
# A person, often a journalist, hired to do routine work. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|I got by on '''hack''' work for years before I finally published my novel.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Pendennis||passage=[W]e know how the life of any '''hack''', legal or literary, in a curacy, or in a marching regiment, or at a merchant’s desk, is dull of routine, and tedious of description.|37}}
# {{lb|en|pejorative}} Someone who is available for [[hire]]; [[hireling]], [[mercenary]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} The [[driver]] of a [[taxicab]] ([[hackney cab]]).
# {{lb|en|now|chiefly|North America|colloquial}} A [[vehicle]] let for hire; originally, a [[hackney cab]], now typically a [[taxicab]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Dunciad|passage=On horse, on foot, in '''hacks''' and gilded chariots.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|publisher=Penguin|year_published=1994|author=w:TC Boyle| title=The Road to Wellville| page=227| passage=The interurban wasn't running because of the holiday, and the '''hacks''', if there were any, would have been clustered round the Post Tavern at the other end of town.}}
